{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgeSmNSxmkE51vPRmsKvt", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I submitted one claim, and it beats the record's mean on dev: **1.1056 bpb against 1.1082**, a gain of about 0.0026. That gain may not count as a formal new record, because the track requires clearing a 0.002 margin at alpha 0.01, and this reaches only p ≈ 0.07.\n\nThat p-value is calculated from the record's stated mean and SD, because its per-seed scores aren't in this copy. I also didn't rerun the record on the full 1024 windows, so 1.1082 is the figure you gave me.\n\n| | Dev bpb | Seed SD | Seeds |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Record, as stated | 1.1082 | 0.0008 | 8 |\n| Claim 1, from `verify.py` | 1.1056 (95% CI ±0.0006) | 0.0008 | 8 fresh |\n\nAll 8 seeds ran without a void or a twin-check failure, and the file in `claims/1/` is byte-identical to the one verified.\n\n**The change:** it's the record entry with one training change. The order 1 table now takes a gradient every 4th step, and orders 2 and 3 every 2nd. On the other steps those tables are read without a gradient. Embedding backward is the largest cost in a training step, and these small tables are visited constantly, so the saved ticks pay for more steps on everything else.\n\n**Reasons to discount the number:**\n- **Selection:** I picked this schedule from 27 variants, comparing them on one seed and a 256-window subset of dev. The 8 final seeds played no part in that choice, but the dev windows did, so the sealed split could come in less favourably.\n- **Noise:** changing PAD alone moved a single-seed score by 0.0015. I only trust the gain because it held on the 8 fresh seeds, and an earlier build with PAD 40 averaged about the same (1.1057) on 4 seeds. Close calls between similar schedules weren't resolved.\n\n**What didn't help:**\n- **Word contexts:** hashed word-prefix tables lost every time, but they always took rows or tables from the order-n set, so the contexts themselves weren't tested in isolation.\n- **Thin rows and heavier skipping:** 4-float high-order rows and more aggressive gradient skipping were also worse.\n- **Tuning:** learning rates, hidden size, batch shape, gate share and update frequency showed no gain beyond that noise. A frozen-table finish, different table orders and moving rows to the order 4 table looked worse.\n\n**Unused time and headroom:**\n- **Lost time:** about 7 minutes went to runs the sandbox killed without my noticing, and about 3 more to a wait that never triggered.\n- **Time left:** I stopped with about 8 minutes remaining, since another round couldn't have been properly confirmed by then.\n- **Eval budget:** the entry uses only about 8,900 of the 50,000 ticks per byte allowed at eval, and I didn't try to use the rest.\n\nI submitted only this one claim, because no other variant had evidence behind it beyond single-seed noise. It's in `claims/1/` (`train.py` and `claim.json`), and the `why` paragraph discloses the selection and the likely miss on the margin."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 2856, "cache_read_input_tokens": 112100, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 2856}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "0b2222de-db9f-412c-8118-8205ac666134", "uuid": "9dcea37e-1e9d-4e09-b869-6fc517a78855", "timestamp": "2026-11-02T16:49:56.715Z", "request_id": "req_011CgeSmN9cAomM9WrqxXQUp"}
